%% include.md.tmpl: the template /respeak:include renders into a repo's static
%% include file (design/include-r1.md); plugin/scripts/respeak-include.py reads it.
%% A line starting with %% is a comment. A line `@@ <section> [name]` opens a
%% section that runs to the next one. {{name}} is a placeholder the script fills.
%% After filling, every paragraph and list item of the body is re-wrapped at 76
%% columns, so the line breaks written here do not reach the output. Each
%% `@@ mode` section holds `reader:`, `cue:` and `contract:` lines;
%% tests/test_include.py pins the mode names to the respeak skill's argument-hint.
%% Any byte changed here makes every rendered file stale (the inputs hash covers
%% this file); bump `@@ version` when the wording changes.
@@ version
1
@@ stamp
<!-- respeak include v{{version}}, inputs {{inputs}}; regenerate with /respeak:include, do not edit -->
@@ body
# How to write in this repo

Two lanes. Notes, packets, tool output and messages between agents are the
machine lane. What a person reads is the human lane: replies to the
user{{files}}.

Machine lane: write in full. {{shorthand}} Never shorten error messages,
security findings, the user's quotes, or numbers with units.

Human lane: write for {{reader}} in {{mode}} mode: {{contract}} When the
user names another reader, switch mode:

{{switches}}

In every mode: short sentences, active voice, concrete words, no filler
openers, no hype, no closing summary of what was just said.

The respeak plugin is optional. With it installed, `/respeak:respeak [mode]`
renders a narrative and its gate checks these rules. Without it, follow
them by hand.
@@ switch
- {{mode}} ({{reader}}, "{{cue}}"): {{contract}}
@@ shorthand lexicon
Use shorthand only for the terms in `.claude/respeak/lexicon-active.md`.
@@ shorthand none
Use no shorthand: this repo ships no lexicon.
@@ mode technical
reader: a peer engineer
cue: technical
contract: the conclusion first, then the evidence as file:line or the command that shows it, then open questions.
@@ mode eli5
reader: a newcomer
cue: plain English
contract: one idea per sentence, at most one analogy, and the next sentence says the real thing.
@@ mode bluf
reader: a manager
cue: bottom line
contract: the result, decision or ask first, with owner and deadline; a manager can forward it unedited.
@@ readers
exec: an executive
peer-engineer: a peer engineer
author: the repo's author
